WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
A safe CORS baseline begins with the same-origin policy.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:09.000
Browsers limit what JavaScript from one origin can read from another origin.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:15.180
CORS relaxes that browser restriction for selected browser clients when the server chooses to allow it.

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.120
That means every exception should be narrow, named, and tied to a real business requirement.

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:27.160
CORS is not authentication, authorization, CSRF protection, or a firewall.

6
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.100
Keep those responsibilities separate.

7
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.080
Authenticate users and services through the appropriate identity mechanisms.

8
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.140
Authorize actions and resources on the server.

9
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.640
Protect state-changing browser workflows.

10
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:45.500
Use CORS only to describe which browser origins may read selected cross-origin responses.

11
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.380
Policies should be explicit and reviewable.

12
00:00:49.480 --> 00:01:03.320
Name the intended frontend and API relationship, scope the policy to endpoints that need it, approve only necessary methods and headers, expose only required response headers, and treat credentialed behavior with extra care.

13
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.180
Development convenience settings should not become production policy.

14
00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.180
Middleware order and endpoint scope matter in ASP.NET Core.

15
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:27.160
CORS behavior depends on how routing, CORS middleware, authentication, authorization, endpoint mapping, response caching, static files, gateways, and proxies fit together.

16
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.660
Preflight behavior should be understood and tested during normal engineering review.

17
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:36.740
The final takeaway is practical: do not widen CORS only to remove a browser error.

18
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:46.280
First identify the intended origin, endpoint, method, headers, credentials, environment, middleware placement, and infrastructure behavior.

19
00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:51.180
Then document the decision and keep it under review as the application changes.
